<Project>

  <!--
    Imported into the CONSUMING app from buildTransitive/<PackageId>.targets. Feeds the keep
    rules packed under proguard/ to R8 when the app builds with Java shrinking on
    (AndroidLinkTool=r8).

    What this protects, precisely: the .NET Android build already auto-generates keep rules for
    every *bound* type and member (obj/.../proguard/proguard_project_references.cfg — verified
    against this package: the full bound surface survives R8 with no help from us). What it
    cannot cover is classes the binding deliberately does not bind — Transforms/Metadata.xml
    removals such as IoT's bundled private RTC/RTM copies, or DSBridge helpers invoked by name
    from JavaScript — when the native/JS side reaches them reflectively, where R8 sees no
    reference. Agora ships exactly these blanket keeps inside its RTC .aars; this carries the
    same practice to the products whose .aars ship none (Signaling, Chat, Whiteboard, IoT) or
    partial rules (Fastboard).

    The include path keys off this file's packaged location (buildTransitive/ next to
    proguard/), so the same shared targets file serves every package that packs rules.

    The function call is deliberately unquoted in the condition: wrapping it in single quotes
    nests quotes ('$(TargetFramework)' inside '…') and MSBuild's condition parser rejects that
    with MSB4092 — in the consuming project, which is the worst place to fail.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="$([MSBuild]::GetTargetPlatformIdentifier('$(TargetFramework)')) == 'android'">
    <ProguardConfiguration Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../proguard/proguard.cfg" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
